---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { localeParams } from '../../lib/i18n';
import { VERSION } from '../../lib/levels';
export const getStaticPaths = localeParams;
const { lang } = Astro.props;
---

<Base lang={lang} path="/project" translated={false}
  title="Project status — AI Usage Scale"
  description="An independent disclosure proposal: its scope, review status, public references, and route toward standards discussion."
  breadcrumb={[["Project status", "/project"]]}>
  <header class="top prose">
    <p class="label">Independent proposal / Definitions v{VERSION}</p>
    <h1>A public vocabulary.<br />An open proposal.</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">AI Usage Scale describes reported use of generative AI in a work.
      It is available for use and implementation while its boundaries and interoperability are open to review.</p>
  </header>
  <div class="project-body prose">
    <section>
      <h2>What the project can claim</h2>
      <p>Six public definitions, a decision procedure, downloadable marks, and machine-readable
        data. The definitions and marks are CC0. The English specification is the normative source;
        the 21 translations are labelled as machine translations awaiting fluent human review.</p>
      <p>A declaration records a publisher's account of the process. It does not establish
        accuracy, quality, copyright, training permission, or compliance with a law. An absent
        declaration leaves the process unknown.</p>
      <p>This is an independent proposal maintained in
        <a href="https://github.com/karauda/ai-usage-scale">the public repository</a> by Konrad Karauda.
        It has not been adopted as a W3C Recommendation or a Schema.org AI disclosure property.
        Suggested mappings to IPTC and C2PA do not imply endorsement.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Public references and use</h2>
      <p>Evidence checked on 10 October 2026:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/chaoss/disclosure#other-ai-disclosureattribution-tools">CHAOSS disclosure</a>
          lists the scale as a related tool. This is a reference, rather than an implementation.</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/chaoss/wg-ai-alignment/blob/main/moderation/README.md#tools">CHAOSS AI Alignment</a>
          includes the scale in its resource list. This is another reference from the same community.</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/voxpelli/diarie/blob/main/.diarie/tasks/tasks-backlog.yml">diarie's public backlog</a>
          contains a deferred adoption task. It records interest and a Level 3 / Level 4 boundary question;
          the entry says that adoption has not yet been implemented.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The audit did not verify an independent live deployment that declares one of these levels.
        Public search cannot rule out private or unindexed uses. References, stars, and example
        classifications are not counted as adopters.</p>
      <p>To document a use, submit a link to an actual declaration through
        <a href="https://github.com/karauda/ai-usage-scale/blob/main/docs/ADOPTERS.md">the adoption evidence file</a>.
        No signature, registration, or account on this site is required to use the scale.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Schema.org and Google</h2>
      <p>The vocabulary can be described using Schema.org's
        <a href="https://schema.org/DefinedTermSet">DefinedTermSet</a> and
        <a href="https://schema.org/DefinedTerm">DefinedTerm</a> types. This describes the vocabulary itself;
        it does not give Google an AI disclosure feature for works carrying a level.</p>
      <p>The next discussion venue is
        <a href="https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/3391">Schema.org issue #3391</a>,
        alongside the <a href="https://www.w3.org/groups/cg/ai-content-disclosure/">W3C AI Content Disclosure Community Group</a>.
        A submission should explain the distinction between original substance and generated form,
        record review separately, and offer real implementation evidence.</p>
      <p>Follow the <a href="https://github.com/karauda/ai-usage-scale/issues/11">public submission and pilot record</a>
        for the current requests, responses and implementation evidence.</p>
      <p>Google lists the structured data features it supports in
        <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/search-gallery">its Search gallery</a>.
        A Schema.org proposal, a valid JSON-LD document, and support in Google Search are separate steps.
        There is no documented dedicated Google Search feature for AI Usage Scale levels.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Review and next steps</h2>
      <p>The October 2026 redesign and content revision were developed with AI. Human editorial
        review is pending. This revision does not automatically declare Level 3 or claim a human
        reviewed every sentence. Translation review is recorded independently.</p>
      <p>The current five-question tree reaches Level 3 before it asks the final human-review
        question. Its Level 3 definition nevertheless requires meaningful review. A publisher
        must check the complete definition; an unreviewed output from original human inputs is
        a boundary that needs a documented decision before changing the classifier.</p>
      <p>Priorities are independent pilot implementations, fluent language reviewers, review of
        ambiguous cases, and a public standards discussion. See the
        <a href="https://github.com/karauda/ai-usage-scale/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md">roadmap</a> and
        <a href="https://github.com/karauda/ai-usage-scale/blob/main/GOVERNANCE.md">governance</a>.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://github.com/karauda/ai-usage-scale/blob/main/docs/history/manifesto-2026-07.md">original July rationale</a>
        is retained as a historical document. The specification defines the current levels.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .top { margin: 4rem 0 3rem; }
  .project-body { margin-inline-start: auto; max-width: 48rem; }
  .project-body li { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
</style>
